{"name":"Wood Nymph","hex":"#eba0a7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wood nymph","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of woodnymph."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood_nymph"}],"entries":[{"title":"Woodnymph","blocks":[{"text":"Woodnymphs are hummingbirds in the genus Thalurania. Males are green and violet-blue, while females are green with white-tipped tails and at least partially whitish underparts. Both sexes have an almost straight, entirely black bill and little or no white post-ocular spot. They are found in forest and tall second growth. The species in this genus are almost entirely allo- or parapatric, and a species is present virtually everywhere in the tropical humid Neotropics."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/ThaluraniaGlaucopis200805DarioSanches.jpg/330px-ThaluraniaGlaucopis200805DarioSanches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodnymph"},{"title":"Cercyonis","blocks":[{"text":"Cercyonis is a genus of butterflies of the subfamily Satyrinae in the family Nymphalidae found in North America. They are commonly called wood-nymphs or wood nymphs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Common_Wood_Nymph.jpg/330px-Common_Wood_Nymph.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cercyonis"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood"},{"title":"Nymph","blocks":[{"text":"A nymph is a minor female nature deity in ancient Greek folklore. Distinct from other Greek goddesses, nymphs are generally regarded as personifications of nature; they are typically tied to a specific place, landform, or tree, and are usually depicted as maidens. Because of their association with springs, they were often seen as having healing properties; other divine powers of the nymphs included divination and shapeshifting. In spite of their divine nature, they were not immortal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Three_nymphs%2C_marble_relief%2C_c._320%E2%80%93300_BC.jpg/330px-Three_nymphs%2C_marble_relief%2C_c._320%E2%80%93300_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nymph"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Cercyonis.jpg/500px-Cercyonis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Cercyonis.jpg/1280px-Cercyonis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Cercyonis.jpg/500px-Cercyonis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cercyonis pegala","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACercyonis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/500px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/1280px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.